/tmp/tmp.fPRw7cudkU/src/ilp.tex:75:In this paper we have investigated whether the intuition of interpreting  ILP solutions with labelled graphs that are MSO definable and of bounded tree-width also applies to the ILP feasibility problem. We have given a positive answer to this question showing that ILP feasibility can indeed be reduced in polynomial time to the satisfiability problem of FO (rather than MSO) on the class of bounded path-width (as opposed to bounded tree-width) graphs which is again decidable by Seese's theorem \cite{Seese91}. What we have not explored yet is whether our approach could also entail the optimal complexity of the problem. Although the ILP feasibility problem is NP-complete, the Boolean programs derived from the automata construction of Section~\ref{automata} only lead to a PSPACE procedure. We believe it is interesting to shed some light in this regards. Furthermore, 
/tmp/tmp.fPRw7cudkU/src/ilp.tex:105:The model-checking problem has been defined in section \ref{logic}. It is possible to define another version of the problem that applies to classes of structures, namely the \textit{model-checking problem for the logic} $\mathcal{L}$ \textit{on the class} $\mathcal{C}$ \textit{of structures} as a decision problem: given a sentence $\varphi \in \mathcal{L}$ and a structure $A \in \mathcal{C}$, decide if $A \models\varphi$. Courcelle's theorem addresses the above problem in the specific case of MSO-logic formulae on graphs of bounded tree width as relational structures, showing that fixed a positive integer $k$, the graph $G$ of tree width $tw(G) = k$ and the MSO formula $\varphi$, it is possible to check whether $G \models \varphi$ in time linear in $|G|$. 
