/tmp/tmp.fPRw7cudkU/src/intro.tex:32:% Recently, Madhusudan and Parlato have shown that the decidability of the emptiness problem for several classes of automata with auxiliary storage (stacks and queues) can uniformly be explained with a common principle \cite{tree-width-popl}. Runs of automata can be naturally represented by labelled graphs with labels from a finite alphabet, such that there exists  a run satisfying a run property $\varphi$ (in this case, if a given state of the automaton is reachable) iff there exists a run satisfying $\varphi$ whose graph representation has bounded tree-width. The tree-width of a graph is a parameter that tells how close to a tree a graph is \cite{Bodlaender}. For any of these classes of automata, the class of graphs representing their runs can be defined in Monadic Second Order logic (MSO), thus, the emptiness problem can be reduced to the satisfiability problem of MSO. In general, MSO satisfiability on graphs is undecidable, but it is decidable on the class of bounded tree-width graphs due to Courcelle/Seese's theorem \cite{courcelle,Seese91}.
/tmp/tmp.fPRw7cudkU/src/intro.tex:36:%In general, the above principle can be lifted to decision problems. Given a decision problem $\cal P$ and an instance $I$ of $\cal P$, the question is whether there is a uniform graph representation for the solutions of $I$ and a bound $B$ such that $I$ admits a positive answer if and only if there is a graph representation of a solution of $I$ whose tree-width is bounded by $B$. For the automata mentioned above, there exists only one graph representation (up to isomorphism) of a ``solution'' (i.e., a run) and this is always of bounded tree-width. Here, we weaken this condition by allowing multiple graph representations for the same solution but at least one with the bounded tree-width property. Although this principle may not be true in general, an interesting direction is that of pursuing a systematic study to establish the generality and the limits of the principle by looking at other decidability results known in the literature. 
