\section{Introduction}
Over the last years, there have been several studies about generic algorithmic techniques aimed at solving classes of problems sharing some common principle. In particular, \cite{Kreutzer08} and \cite{Grohe08} review interesting results known as algorithmic meta-theorems. Such meta-theorems are of deep significance in the context of both parameterized and descriptive complexity theory as they form a bridge between these two theories by relating classes of logic expressions, structures and complexity. As reminded by \cite{Grohe08}, the typical form of an algorithmic meta-theorem follows a common template, that is: ``a class of problems definable using a certain \textit{logic} on a certain class of \textit{structures} can be solved \textit{efficiently}'', where the term \textit{efficiently} may correspond to several meanings, such as fixed-parameter tractability, polynomial-time computability and so on. In the case of fixed-parameter tractability, for instance, several techniques are discussed in \cite{Parameterized} to solve problems efficiently for small values of the parameter. One possibility is to identify a class of logic formulae to describe the problem and some characteristic of the structure that allow fixed-parameter tractability when considered to be the parameter. 


