    13	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:pw}
    14	For each solution $\vec{s}$ of $A\vec{x}=\vec{b}$ with $n$ variables, 
    15	there is a graph $G \in {\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ whose path-width 
    16	is upper-bounded by $2n$.
    17	%\vspace{-1eX}
    18	\end{lemma}
    19	\begin{proof}
    20	For each solution $\vec{s}$, 
    21	${\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$
    22	may contain several graphs $G$ with $\vec{s}=sol(G)$. 
    23	All of those have the same number of vertices with the same label as well 
    24	as the same number of edges with the same label. Here, we show that among all graphs in 
    25	${\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ representing $\vec{s}$, 
    26	there exists one whose path-width is bounded by $2n$.
    27	Without loss of generality we assume that $\vec{s}$ is a reduced solution,
    28	i.e. it is not a multiple of another solution and that $\vec{s}$ does not
    29	contain $0$.
    30	
    31	Let $\vec{s} = (s_1, \dots, s_n)$.
    32	The proof is given by fixing $\vec{b}=\vec{0}$. 
    33	In this case the path-width can be bounded by $2n-1$.
    34	At the end we show how to generalize the proof to any $\vec{b}$.
    35	Any graph $G\in {\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ with $sol(G)=\vec{s}$ 
    36	has $s_i$ vertices labelled by $i$. 
    37	We say that $G \in {\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$, with $G = (V,E,\{V_i\}_{i\in[0,n]},\{E_j\}_{j\in[1,m]})$,
    38	is in {\em special form} if it satisfies the following two conditions:
    39	(1) there is a partition $\{V^k\}_{k \in [1,t]}$ of $V$
    40	such that no two vertices of $V^k$ are labelled with the same
    41	variable index (i.e. $|V^k \cap V_i| \leq 1$ for all $i,k$), and (2) there is a partition
    42	$\{E^k\}_{k \in [1,t]}$ of $E$ such that for all $k$,
    43	all edges in $E^1\cup\ldots\cup E^k$ only relate vertices in
    44	$V^1\cup\ldots\cup V^k$.
    45	
    46	We now define a path decomposition for $G$ in special form.
    47	We say that a vertex $v$ in $V$ in a subgraph of $G$ is fully matched if 
    48	the subgraph contains all edges of $G$ involving $v$.
    49	The bags of the path decomposition of $G$ are given 
    50	by the sequence $B_p^0,B_p^1, B_p^2, \dots, B_p^{t}$. 
    51	Initially, $B_p^0 = \emptyset$. At each step $k$, 
    52	$B_p^{k+1} = B_p^k \setminus \{ v \in B_p^{k}  \mid v \textrm{ is fully matched in }
    53	(V^1\cup\cdots\cup V^k,E^1\cup\cdots \cup E^k)\} \cup V^{k+1}$.
    54	
    55	It is clear that the linear graph whose vertices are the bags
    56	$B_p^1, \dots,  B_p^k$ is a path decomposition for $G$.
    57	The size of the bags depends on the particular partition of vertices and
    58	edges.
