   280	Next, we show that every graph in ${\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ defines a solution of $A\vec{x}=\vec{b}$ and vice-versa. Let $sol:{\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]\rightarrow \mathbb{N}^n$ be a function that associates to every graph $G$ in ${\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ a vector of natural numbers representing the number of vertices labelled with $i$, for each $i\in [1,n]$, i.e., $sol(G)=(|V_1|,\ldots,|V_n|)$.
   281	
   282	\begin{proposition}\label{prop:graphs}
   283	The image of the function $sol:{\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]\rightarrow \mathbb{N}^n$ is exactly the set of all solutions of $A\vec{x}=\vec{b}$.
   284	%For each solution $\vec{s}\in \mathbb{N}^n$ of $A\vec{x}=\vec{b}$ there exists a graph $G$ in $G[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ such that $sol(G)=\vec{s}$ and for each graph $G$ in $G[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ there exists 
   285	\end{proposition}
   286	\begin{proof}
   287	Let $A\vec{x}=\vec{b}$ be an ILP instance and $G=(V,E,\{V_i\}_{i\in[0,n]},\{E_j\}_{j\in[1,m]})$ be a graph in ${\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$. We show that for every $j\in [1,m]$, $sol(G)=(|V_1|,\ldots,|V_n|)$ is a solution of the equation $a_{j,1}\cdot x_1+\ldots +a_{j,n}\cdot x_n = b_j$. Let $a_{j,0}=-b_j$. The set of indices $[0,n]$ can be partitioned in two sets $\{p_1,\ldots,p_s\}$ and  $\{n_1,\ldots,n_t\}$ s.t. 
   288	for every $k\in[1,s]$, $a_{j,p_k}$ is positive and for every $k\in[1,t]$, $a_{j,n_k}$ is negative. By definition, all the edges of $G$ labelled by $j$ are between a vertex in $V_{p_1}\cup\ldots\cup V_{p_s}$ and a vertex in $V_{n_1}\cup\ldots\cup V_{n_t}$. Also, for every $i\in [0,n]$, the degree of every vertex in $V_i$ equals $|a_{j,i}|$ and thus the number of edges labelled by $j$ can be written as both
   289	\[
   290	|V_{p_1}|\cdot a_{j,p_1} +\ldots + |V_{p_s}|\cdot a_{j,p_s}\mbox{ and }|V_{n_1}|\cdot |a_{j,n_1}| +\ldots + |V_{n_t}|\cdot |a_{j,n_t}|,
   291	\]
   292	which proves that $sol(G)$ is a solution of $a_{j,1}\cdot x_1+\ldots +a_{j,n}\cdot x_n = b_j$.
   293	
   294	For the reverse, we show that for every solution $\vec{s}=(s_i)_{i\in[1,n]}$ of $A\vec{x}=\vec{b}$, there exists a graph $G=(V,E,\{V_i\}_{i\in[0,n]},\{E_j\}_{j\in[1,m]})$ in ${\mathcal G}[A\vec{x}=\vec{b}]$ s.t. $sol(G)=\vec{s}$. Therefore, for every $i\in [1,n]$, the set $V_i$ consists of $s_i$ vertices. Then, for every equation $a_{j,1}\cdot x_1+\ldots a_{j,n}\cdot x_n = b_j$ we consider the partition of $[0,n]$ into $\{p_1,\ldots,p_s\}$ and  $\{n_1,\ldots,n_t\}$ exactly as above. We also consider that $a_{j,0}=-b_j$ and $s_0=1$. The fact that $\vec{s}$ is a solution implies that 
   295	\[
   296	s_{p_1} \cdot a_{j,p_1} +\ldots + s_{p_s}\cdot a_{j,p_s}=s_{n_1}\cdot |a_{j,n_1}| +\ldots + s_{n_t}\cdot |a_{j,n_t}|,
   297	\]
   298	which shows that it is possible to define a multi-set of edges $E_j$ satisfying the constraints in Definition~\ref{ILPgraphs}.
   299	\end{proof}
