    23	
    24	In this paper, we consider the {\em feasibility problem} for {\em integer linear programming} (ILP, for short) that asks whether, given a finite set $I$ of linear constraints, there is an assignment of its variables such that all the constraints are satisfied\footnote{W.l.o.g., we suppose that the variables are interpreted as  positive integers and that $I$ contains only equalities.}. We show that the decidability principle based on bounded tree-width graph representations applies to the ILP feasibility problem in a stronger sense as described below.
    25	
    26	%and, for every $i \in [1..n]$, the number of vertices labelled by $x_i$ equals the value of the variable $x_i$ in the solution. 
    27	As a {\bf first contribution} we give a natural graph representation for the solutions of an instance $I$ of ILP. The nodes of the graph represent a unary encoding of the solution, i.e. each node is labelled with exactly one variable of $I$, and the number of nodes with the same label is the value of the corresponding variable in the solution. The edges are used to enforce the constraints of the system. For simplicity, consider a system with only one constraint, where each variable is associated with one coefficient. Depending on the sign of this coefficient each variable can contribute to the overall value of the constraint by either increasing or decreasing it. Each node will have a number of edges equal to the absolute value of the corresponding coefficient. We use edges to pair nodes whose corresponding coefficients have different signs. Thus, a graph with well-matched nodes is a solution. In case of multiple constraints, we reiterate the above mechanism for each constraint individually, labelling the edges with the constraint represented. Since multiple ``matchings'' are possible for the same solution, a solution may have several of those graphs representing it. We prove that the class of graphs representing the solutions of an instance $I$ can be defined in first-order logic. See Figure~\ref{fig:ex_graph} for an example of a solution for a two-constraints system. 
    28	
    29	% with a first order formula on graphs. % $\varphi_I$.
    30	%all these properties for an instance $I$ can be encoded with a first order formula on graphs $\varphi_I$ that logically characterizes all ``graph solutions'' of an instance $I$.
    31	
    32	% Recently, Madhusudan and Parlato have shown that the decidability of the emptiness problem for several classes of automata with auxiliary storage (stacks and queues) can uniformly be explained with a common principle \cite{tree-width-popl}. Runs of automata can be naturally represented by labelled graphs with labels from a finite alphabet, such that there exists  a run satisfying a run property $\varphi$ (in this case, if a given state of the automaton is reachable) iff there exists a run satisfying $\varphi$ whose graph representation has bounded tree-width. The tree-width of a graph is a parameter that tells how close to a tree a graph is \cite{Bodlaender}. For any of these classes of automata, the class of graphs representing their runs can be defined in Monadic Second Order logic (MSO), thus, the emptiness problem can be reduced to the satisfiability problem of MSO. In general, MSO satisfiability on graphs is undecidable, but it is decidable on the class of bounded tree-width graphs due to Courcelle/Seese's theorem \cite{courcelle,Seese91}.
    33	
    34	%Among the problems that have been shown decidable using this principle we have: (1) state reachability problem \cite{tree-width-popl}, model-checking of LTL \cite{ahmedLTL,scopedFSTTCS}, and {\em generalised} LTL logics \cite{salvatoreLOGIC} for various restrictions of multi-stack pushdown automata \cite{boundedCS,phase,faouziORDERED,salvatoreSCOPED}, and (2) the reachability problem~\cite{tree-width-popl,Heussner} for subclasses of distributed automata that communicate through unbounded FIFO channels~\cite{gennaroTACAS08,muschollQUEUE}. The surprising aspect is that the new proofs are uniform and radically different from the ones previously proposed in literature, which are specifically crafted using different techniques on a case-by-case basis. This strengthens the intuition that a common principle governs the decidability of (those) problems. Furthermore, the same principle has also been used to derive new decidability results, for other restrictions of multi-stack pushdown automata \cite{tree-width-popl} and fragments of separation logic \cite{Radu}.
