    41	
    42	% As a {\bf first contribution} we give a graph representation for the solutions of an instance $I$ of the ILP problem. The vertices of the graph represent an unary encoding of the solution, i.e., each vertex is labelled with exactly one variable of $I$, and, for every $i \in [1..n]$, the number of vertices labelled by $x_i$ equals the value of the variable $x_i$ in the solution. Each edge is labelled with the index of a constraint of $I$ (assuming a total order on the constraints). For any edge labelled with $j$, the variables labelling its endpoints are multiplied in the $j$th constraint by some coefficients of opposite signs, i.e., if $u$ and $v$ are the endpoints of the edge, $u$ is labelled with $x_{i_1}$, and $v$ is labelled with $x_{i_2}$, then $a_{j,i_{1}}$ and $a_{j,i_{2}}$ have opposite signs, where $a_{j,i}$ is the coefficient of $x_{i}$ in the $j$th constraint. Furthermore, the number of edges labelled with $j$ incident to a vertex labelled with $x_i$ is exactly $|a_{j,i}|$. We show that if these restrictions hold for all $j$-edges then the $j$th constraint of $I$ is satisfied. Intuitively, the edges labelled with $j$ are used to match unary digits from the integer values of a solution according to $j$th constraint. Since multiple ``matchings'' are possible for the same solution, a solution may have several graph representations. We prove that the class of graphs representing solutions of an instance $I$ can be defined in first-order logic. 
    43	
    44	In general, the class of graphs representing all solutions may have unbounded path-width. We show that, for any solution, there always exists a graph representing it of {\em path-width} at most $2n$, where $n$ is the number of variables of $I$, and this constitutes the {\bf second contribution} of the paper. %although not all graph representations have bounded tree-width,
    45	The path-width of a graph measures its closeness to a path (rather than a tree, as for tree-width).  
    46	This provides us with a restriction of the decidability principle outlined above for the case of ILP, where bounded path-width is already sufficient as opposed to the general case where the tree-width needs to be bounded. 
    47	
    48	%If we are concerned 
    49	% Other results from the literature could be used to obtain similar results. The exponential bound on the minimal solutions of an ILP instance~\cite{Papadimitriou81} implies that, for any feasible ILP instance $I$, there exists a bound $B$ of size exponential in the size of $I$, such that some (but not all) solutions have a graph representation of path-width bounded by $B$. Our result states that there exists a bounded path-width graph representation for \emph{all} solutions of an ILP instance $I$ and the bound depends only on the number of variables of $I$. Also, if we consider a graph representation based on a binary encoding of the solutions of an ILP instance (i.e., nodes are labelled by bits and edges link bits coming from the same integer), then the path-width of the graph representation of a solution is bounded by a constant. This is implied by the fact that the binary addition can be described by a regular language and one can construct a finite automaton to represent the binary encodings of all the solutions of an ILP instance~\cite{Wolper95}. In principle, our result based on unary encodings could be extended to decide the non-emptiness of an intersection between a set of linear constraints and other types of constraints (e.g., regular languages~\footnote{i.e., if the Parikh image of a regular language satisfies a set of linear constraints}), which is not  possible if we consider binary encodings.
